Date: Tue, 15 Jun 1993 15:38:23 -0800
From: lefty@apple.com (Lefty)
Subject: Pregnant dolls and Rambo Action Figures

>It seems unlikely that girls are genetically attracted
>to fashion and boys are attracted to weapons? It is possible that we
>are still practicing some sort of genetic behavior that prepped
>boys for hunting and girls for homemaking?

I've been watching this thread with a growing degree of amusement, since it
seems fairly clear to me that the number of people making rash statements
about the way small children behave have little actual exposure to
children.  Rather, they seem to be making judgements based on the way in
which toy companies attempt, not necessarily successfully, to market to
children.

Case in point:  I was spending some time recently with a four and a half
year old girl of my acquaintance.  We were, not surprisingly, watching TV,
specifically "Rug Rats", a cartoon she enjoys and I fail to find obnoxious.

A commercial for G.I. Joe Action figures comes on.  "I want that!" says my
friend.  A commercial for Kitty Surprise (a stuffed cat toy with baby
kittens inside it) follows.  "I want that!" she repeats.

What does she play with?  Teddy bears.  Legos.  A fire truck.  A Barbie
doll.  A doll house.  A motorcycle.

A fairly eclectic range here.  Note that her parents generally let her
choose her own toys, rather than picking things for her.

I'd suggest that if children do, on occasion, manifest some tilt toward one
genre of amusement (i.e. "boy toys" versus "girl toys"), it is because they
are mirroring some set of expectations on the part of their parents.

