Date: Tue, 15 Jun 93 16:01:54 -0700
From: Valerie Lambert <biocad!valis.biocad.com!valerie@sgi.com.UUCP>
Subject: Sex [and other physical] differences: Who's talking?

Frothing feminist--Not.  If there are any two groups with issues to
resolve, then both groups are responsible for negotiating and resolving
them.  Neither can sit back and expect the other to fix things.

Of course, I didn't mean to invoke demons by my message mentioning habitual
patterns of male/female interaction.  Nobody needs more knee-jerk arguments
about it.  My intended point was that it is a Good Thing for all
participants in a conversation to be aware how old communication patterns
shape the event.  I don't want the men to lean back in their chairs, and
politely invite the meek women to speak.  I don't want the women to demand
attention and respect if their contribution does not warrant it.  Just
increasing awareness, that will help shape the integrity of future
meetings.

Let's generalize for a moment.  My true desire is that physical diversity
of any kind (sex, race, weight, disability, disfigurement, visible implant,
attire, etc.) would not adversely color technical meetings.  However, they
still do, to some degree, in many of the meetings I participate in.  Partly
because diversity is not universally tolerated.  Partly because the
minority (of any type in a given meeting) often expect a problem or special
treatment.

Given the nature of this list, it seems that many of you _already_ base
your respect to other people on their non-physical/non-visible attributes.
Bravo!  But it can't hurt to give further consideration to the matter,
given that we, as Extropians, will certainly face discrimination issues as
we [hopefully] change--or entirely shed--our existing bodies.  Even if we
exalt in our fine new forms, if we still have emotional capacity, then we
will feel the ache of rejection from our friends, family, lovers, and
colleagues who find our intimate embrace with technology repugnant
and perverse.

We don't have to wait for uploading or nanomumble to start experiencing
this.  God[dess] forbid that I would want to _wear_ a computer!  How many
of you find the trade-off of instant access v.s. social stigma acceptable
with, say, a HipPC and PrivateEye?  I don't think I have the guts.  Maybe
later, when it is less obtrusive, more powerful, at least marginally
fashionable somewhere.

