From: Andrew S Hall <ashall@magnus.acs.ohio-state.edu>
Subject: Automated Mail Harassment

I am an infrequent poster and also got a re-quoting warning. I have
a personal policy of not reading any message that scrolls through the
first two screens with no new text.

I have a problem with this numerical analysis of re-quotation. This list
(and the Usenet to a much bigger degree) too often has the character of
sound bites. People try to zing off short and fast snippets in response
to others doing the same. When someone has something substantive to say
and someone else has a substantive objection, neither are served by merely
re-quoting a pair of lines and then writing a 200 line rebuttal. This
leads to confusion, acrimony, and then pissing contests.

In technical arguments, requoting a whole paragraph may be essential to
capture precisely what the person said. The rule about excessive
re-quoting must be taken in context for each post. Re-quoting even
one line of "Die Vegan slime in a heap of putrefying vegtables." is
excessive. Re-quoting twenty lines of an explanation of a crypto scheme
or a mathematical theorem is not.

I am willing to wade through reasonable levels of requotations in order to
avoid having just posts and sound bite responses. I also think that
we should add an automatic prefixer to the software for one header:
>>>>> for re-re-re-re-re-re-re-re-quotations.

